 are moving to Berlin  there are now at least 40,000 expats  from english-speaking countries in the  city there's even a thriving expat scene  with its own cafes and clubs some  newcomers organize themselves on online  forums there are cultural magazines by  and for expats English language book  shops and even an annual cultural  festival organised by the newcomers I  come from France I'm staying here for a  year and I want to stay longer I'm from  Scotland I've been here about three and  a half years I wanted to come and stay  for 90 days on a tourist visa and learn  a little bit of German and just really  liked it here and decided to stay like  you have the benefits of being a big  city there's so much going on but it's  not got like the crazy rush that you  have in London or in New York it's  burning you're living in the you're  living in the time it's where things are  happening at the moment it's not in any  other place in Europe here there is  enough space to be and to do whatever  you want  the new arrivals have a tight network  and often live in a bubble without much  contact with native Berliners many of  them don't even speak German certain  areas in english is actually the most  common language which is why you get  places like this where the staff are  mostly Australian and speak English to  each other all the time because you do  hear English Australian Swedish rather  than German which is weird and but when  you step outside the doors I mean we're  in oh can you step outside and you're  instantly back into Germany James  glazebrook and Zoe noble have resolved  to make learning German their top  priority they even like Berlin so much  they're thinking of staying for good
 

